     "result_text_tail": "second-half renewals, worth up to +$0.7M.\n  - No increase for the promotion group, plus an onboarding programme.\n- **Against the objectives.** The plan adds $4.3M from price and keeps net revenue retention near 114%. Existing customers alone grow ARR 14.2%; about $0.6M from new customers reaches 15%. On paper it brings in $0.8\u20132.0M less than the CFO's plan, and the deck says so.\n- **Two claims the deck pushes back on.** \"Halve SMB churn\" would mean 1.55%, which is below any SMB level since 2023. A realistic target is about 2.1%, which keeps up to roughly $1.0M of revenue. The board dashboard's \"1.4% \u2192 2.3%\" can't be reproduced from the segment data under any obvious definition.\n\n**Choices I made on your behalf** (also listed in the appendix):\n- **Mid-market at 10%, not 12%:** 10% is the level the 2024 test covered.\n- **SMB test at +8%:** this is the 2023 increase, so that year's reaction is a usable comparison.\n- **Enterprise uncapped contracts at up to +10%.**\n- **Enterprise contracts renewing in 2027 assumed 60% capped,** the same share as the whole enterprise book.\n- **2027 retention before price assumed equal to the last 12 months.**\n- **Renewals assumed spread evenly through the year.**\n- **Not modelled because the pack doesn't cover them:** new-customer sales, margin and the cost of the onboarding programme. Hitting 15% depends on new-customer sales, so the deck asks leadership to confirm that number.\n\nFiles are in `./deliverable/`:\n- Palisade_2027_pricing_decision.pptx"
    }
